  "full_response": "Observability in software systems refers to the ability to understand and measure how a system is performing, especially in terms of its internal states, health, and functionality. Observability encompasses the use of various metrics, logs, and traces to gain insights into software behavior, aiming to pinpoint failures and bottlenecks, and to encourage proactive remediation.\n\nMonitoring, on the other hand, is a subset of observability. Monitoring focuses on predefined events, metrics, and health checks to collect data from a system. These predefined parameters are typically associated with known performance indicators, error rates, or system throughput. Monitoring is about keeping an eye on these specific aspects and often involves alerting the right personnel when certain thresholds are crossed or when trends deviate from normal behavior.\n\nObservability is a broader concept that aims to make inferred actions, side-effects, and the entire picture of a complex system comprehensible. It includes monitoring, logging, and much more, essentially extending beyond just the defined metrics to give insights that are not initially anticipated. With regards to improving traceability and understanding, observability needs a combination of telemetry methods and a deeper analysis that enables engineers and analysts to diagnose issues and comprehend the system's flow of execution, particularly in distributed systems, which might involve multiple microservices, data pipelines, and other complex infrastructures.",
